/**
@page scripting_guide Scripting Guide

@tableofcontents

Game behaviour is written in [AngelScript](https://www.angelcode.com/angelscript/)
files under `scripts/`. The engine compiles every `.as` file in-process when
you build or play a project. There is no separate bundler, source map or
language server: a compile error fails **Build Scripts** / **Play** and is
printed in the Console.

Gameplay can also be written in C++ with the same callbacks, fields, Events
and map format. The C++ side of both systems is @ref Mercury::Behaviour (the
base class the engine calls) and @ref Mercury::MapScene (the scene that owns
the objects). See @ref getting_started_cpp for a complete C++ example.

@section scripting_file A script file

@code{.cpp}
// scripts/player.as
[script]
class Player : Behaviour
{
    [field tooltip="Units per second"]
    float speed = 8.0f;

    void onStart()
    {
        Debug::Log("Player ready");
    }

    void onTick(double dt)
    {
        GameObject self = GetGameObject();
        Vector3 move = Vector3(1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f) * speed * dt;
        self.position = self.position + move;
    }
}
@endcode

- A class marked `[script]` is registered as a component. Its map name is
  the class name; `[script "Name"]` overrides it.
- A file may hold several classes.
- A class marked `[dataAsset]` is a ScriptableObject-style asset type: it
  appears in the **Create** menu and is saved as a `.masset`.
- Add the component in the Inspector (**Add Component**), or write
  `[component type="Player" node="Player"]` in a `.mmap`.

@section scripting_lifecycle Lifecycle

The names are Unity's, in camelCase. Define only the callbacks you need.

| Callback | When it runs |
|---|---|
| `awake()` | As soon as the object exists; every object in a loaded map exists by then. May run for a disabled script. |
| `onEnable()` | When the script becomes enabled on an active object. |
| `onDisable()` | When it stops being enabled or active. |
| `onStart()` | Once, before the first tick or frame for which the script is enabled. |
| `onFrame(double dt)` | Every rendered frame. `dt` is in seconds. |
| `onLateFrame(double dt)` | Every frame after every script's `onFrame` (Unity's `LateUpdate`). |
| `onTick(double dt)` | Every fixed tick, before physics. Use this for forces, movement and simulation. |
| `onCollisionEnter/Stay/Leave(GameObject)` | Physics contacts. |
| `onTriggerEnter/Stay/Exit(GameObject)` | Trigger overlaps. |
| `onDestroy()` | When the object is destroyed. |

Inside `onTick`, `Time::FixedDeltaTime()` is the fixed step. Do not call
engine functions from a constructor: the object and its components do not
exist yet. Initialise plain fields in the constructor and everything else in
`awake()` or `onStart()`.

Collisions are reported at the start of the next tick. A script that is
disabled receives no `onStart`, `onFrame` or `onTick`.

@section scripting_fields Inspector fields

`[field]` exposes a public or private field to the Inspector and saves it in
the map:

@code{.cpp}
[field tooltip="World units per second"]
float speed = 60.0f;

[field] int lives = 3;
[field] string label = "Score";
[field] Vector3 spawn = Vector3(0.0f, 1.0f, 0.0f);
[field] Color tint = Color(1.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f, 1.0f);
[field] array<int> scores;
[field] Event onDefeated;
@endcode

Supported field types are `bool`, `int`, `float`, `string`, `Vector2`,
`Vector3`, `Color`, `Event` and `array<T>` of those. The map key is the field
name exactly (`minHorizontal`, not `min_horizontal`). A key that no field has
is a warning when the project is built.

Other metadata:

| Metadata | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `[field tooltip="..."]` | An Inspector field with a tooltip. |
| `[enum "a", "b"]` | Show an `int` as a drop-down; the map stores the name. |
| `[nodeRef]` | A `string` edited as a node path. |
| `[asset "Material"]` | A `string` edited as an asset path of the given type. |
| `[bindable]` on `get_x()` | A computed value UI bindings may read; not saved or shown in the Inspector. |
| `[callable]` | A method an `Event` may call. Its argument may be none, `bool`, `int`, `float` or `string`. |
| `[dataAsset]` | A ScriptableObject-style asset type. |

@section scripting_objects Objects and the scene

`GetGameObject()` returns a `GameObject`, a lightweight handle to the object
that owns the script. Its properties are `name`, `path`, `tag`, `layer`,
`activeSelf`, `activeInHierarchy`, `parent`, `children`, `position`,
`rotation`, `eulerAngles`, `localScale`, `velocity`, `isValid`, `hasBounds`,
`boundsMin` and `boundsMax`. Methods include `FindChild`, `SetParent` and
`Destroy`.

@code{.cpp}
GameObject self = GetGameObject();
self.position = Vector3(0.0f, 2.0f, 0.0f);
self.eulerAngles = Vector3(0.0f, 90.0f, 0.0f);

GameObject left_wall = Scene::Find("Walls/Left");
GameObject player = Scene::FindWithTag("Player");
@endcode

`Scene` creates and loads:

@code{.cpp}
GameObject bullet = Scene::CreateGameObject("Bullet", GetGameObject());
GameObject enemy = Scene::Instantiate(prefab, position, rotation, parent);
Scene::LoadMap("res://maps/level2.mmap");   // applied at the next frame
Scene::Quit();
@endcode

`GetGameObject().isValid` is false after `Destroy()`. Using a destroyed
object throws `GameObject was destroyed`; check `isValid` if another script
may have destroyed it.

@section scripting_vectors Vectors and math

`Vector2`, `Vector3`, `Quaternion` and `Color` are native value types. Assign
whole values instead of mutating components:

@code{.cpp}
Vector3 position = GetGameObject().position;
Vector3 next = position + Vector3(0.0f, 1.0f, 0.0f) * speed * dt;
GetGameObject().position = next;

float length = next.Length();
Vector3 direction = next.Normalized();
float dot = a.Dot(b);
Vector3 cross = a.Cross(b);
@endcode

`Mathf`, `Random` and `Time` follow Unity's names: `Mathf::Clamp`,
`Mathf::Lerp`, `Mathf::Deg2Rad`, `Mathf::Sqrt`, `Random::Range`,
`Random::Value`, `Time::DeltaTime`, `Time::FixedDeltaTime`.

@section scripting_components Components

`Components` reads and writes a component by type name:

@code{.cpp}
Component body = Components::Get(GetGameObject(), "Rigidbody");
if (!body.isValid)
    Components::Add(GetGameObject(), "Rigidbody");

float mass = body.GetFloat("mass");
body.SetFloat("mass", 2.0f);
@endcode

The generic accessors are `GetFloat`/`SetFloat`, `GetBool`/`SetBool`,
`GetInt`/`SetInt`, `GetString`/`SetString`, `GetVector2`/`SetVector2`,
`GetVector3`/`SetVector3` and `GetColor`/`SetColor`. Typed helpers cover
common components: `AnimatorSetFloat`, `AnimatorPlay`, `AudioPlay`,
`AudioPlayOneShot`, `AudioSetFloat`, `CharacterMove` and `CharacterGrounded`.

Other scripts are found by type:

@code{.cpp}
IBehaviour@ other = GetBehaviour(GetGameObject(), "Health");
Health@ health = cast<Health>(other);
if (health !is null) health.TakeDamage(10.0f);
@endcode

@section scripting_physics Physics

@code{.cpp}
Physics::AddForce(GetGameObject(), Vector3(0.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f), ForceMode::Force);
Physics::AddImpulse(GetGameObject(), Vector3(0.0f, 5.0f, 0.0f));
Physics::AddTorque(GetGameObject(), Vector3(0.0f, 1.0f, 0.0f));

bool hit = Physics::Raycast(origin, direction, max_distance, layer_mask,
                            query_triggers, hit_object, point, normal,
                            distance);
@endcode

Use `onTick` for forces and impulses: `onFrame` runs at the display rate,
while physics advances at the project's fixed tick rate. For a character,
add a `CharacterController` and call `CharacterMove(displacement)`; check
`CharacterGrounded()` before jumping. A `Rigidbody` controls static,
kinematic or dynamic motion, mass, locked axes, damping and gravity;
`BoxCollider`, `SphereCollider`, `CapsuleCollider`, `CylinderCollider`,
`PlaneCollider` and `MeshCollider` define the shapes. A collider with
`is_trigger` reports overlaps without pushing.

@section scripting_input Input

Keyboard and mouse:

@code{.cpp}
if (Input::IsKeyPressed(Key::W))       { /* held */ }
if (Input::IsKeyJustPressed(Key::Space)) { /* pressed this frame */ }
if (Input::IsMouseButtonPressed(MouseButton::Left)) { /* ... */ }

Vector2 delta = Input::CursorDelta();
float wheel = Input::Scroll();
bool over_ui = Input::IsPointerOverUI();
@endcode

Projects may define input actions in a `.minput` asset (Unity's Input System).
Use the generated constants from **Window > Input Actions** with the
**Generate AngelScript** option enabled:

@code{.cpp}
private InputAction move;
private InputAction jump;
private bool jump_requested = false;

void onStart()
{
    move = Input::RequireAction(GameInput::Player::Move);
    jump = Input::RequireAction(GameInput::Player::Jump);
}

void onFrame(double dt)
{
    if (jump.wasPressedThisFrame) jump_requested = true;
}

void onTick(double dt)
{
    Vector2 value = move.ReadVector2();
    Vector3 direction(value.x, 0.0f, -value.y);
    if (direction.SqrLength() != 0.0f)
        Physics::AddForce(GetGameObject(), direction.Scale(force), 0);

    if (jump_requested) { /* jump */ }
    jump_requested = false;
}
@endcode

Actions update once per frame, but a frame may run zero or several fixed
ticks. Read a press in `onFrame` and remember it for `onTick`, as above.
`Input::RequireAction` fails the script at `onStart` if the action does not
exist, which is why the generated constants are recommended.

@section scripting_ui UI Toolkit

In-game UI is built with `.uxml` documents and `.uss` style sheets, and shown
by a `UIDocument` component. From a script:

@code{.cpp}
void onStart()
{
    VisualElement root = UI::Root();
    if (!root.isValid) return;

    VisualElement score = root.Q("score");
    if (score.isValid) score.SetText("0");

    VisualElement pause = root.Q("pause");
    if (pause.isValid) pause.OnClick("Pause");
}

void Pause()
{
    Debug::Log("Paused");
}
@endcode

Useful calls are `Q("name")` / `QClass("class")`, `SetText`, `get_text` /
`set_text`, `SetClass("name", on)`, `HasClass`, `Focus`, `BlurFocused`,
`SetDisplay`, `OnClick("Method")` (optionally with one string argument),
`OnToggleChanged("Method")` and `SetDataSource()`.

Callbacks are method names, not closures: keep per-callback state in fields.
`UI::Root()` returns null when the object has no `UIDocument`.

To bind values, mark the values you want as `[field]` or `[bindable]` and
call `SetDataSource()` on the root. The UXML binding's
`data-source-path` is the field or getter name:

@code{.cpp}
[field] int score = 0;

[bindable]
int get_time()
{
    return int(Time::Now());
}

void onStart()
{
    UI::Root().SetDataSource();
}
@endcode

`[field]` values are also saved in the map and shown in the Inspector;
`[bindable]` values are computed and are not saved. See
@ref Mercury::Behaviour::ReflectData and
@ref Mercury::Behaviour::AsDataSource for the C++ equivalent.

@section scripting_events Events

An `Event` field is a UnityEvent. The map saves a list of calls to other
scripts' `[callable]` methods:

@code{.cpp}
[field] Event entered;

void onTriggerEnter(GameObject other)
{
    entered.Invoke();
}

// In another script on another object:
[callable]
void TakeDamage(float amount)
{
    health -= amount;
}
@endcode

In the Inspector, add a call to the event, pick the target object and choose
the method and argument. A method does not need to be `[callable]` to be
called directly from a script you already have a handle to.

@section scripting_errors Errors and debugging

`Debug::Log`, `Debug::Warn`, `Debug::Error` and `Debug::Info` write to the
Console. A script that throws does not stop the game: the error includes its
`scripts/file.as:line:column` location, and the next callback still runs.

A call that exceeds `MERCURY_SCRIPT_TIMEOUT` seconds (10 by default; `0`
disables the check) is aborted as an infinite loop. Scripts hold only opaque
handles (a scene serial and an entity id); the engine compiles AngelScript
with unsafe references disabled and registers no raw pointers, so a bad
script cannot crash the engine.

The editor never runs game code. After **Build Scripts** it runs
`mercury_player --dump-schema .mercury/scripts.mschema` to learn the fields
of every script. That generated folder is not committed.

@section scripting_cpp C++ scripts

A C++ script derives from @ref Mercury::Behaviour, describes its fields in
`Reflect`, and registers itself with `MERCURY_SCRIPT`. The files live in the
project's `src/` folder (the Project Browser's **Create > Script...** dialog
can write the pair for you):

@code{.cpp}
// src/spinner.h
#pragma once
#include <scripting/behaviour.h>

class Spinner : public Mercury::Behaviour {
 public:
  void Reflect(Mercury::Reflector& r) override {
    r.Field("speed", speed).Tooltip("Degrees per second");
  }
  void OnFrame(double dt) override {
    GetTransform().ChangeRotate(
        {0.0f, speed * static_cast<float>(dt), 0.0f});
  }
  float speed = 45.0f;
};
@endcode

@code{.cpp}
// src/spinner.cc
#include "spinner.h"
MERCURY_SCRIPT(Spinner);
@endcode

The class can be used from a map exactly like an AngelScript `[script]` class:

@code{.ini}
[component type="Spinner" node="Cube"]
speed = 45.0
@endcode

The callback names are the C++ spellings of the AngelScript ones, and
`Reflect` supports the same field types as `[field]`. `MERCURY_DATA_ASSET(Type)`
registers a reflected struct as a `.masset` type. Both languages can be used
in one project, and a node can mix a C++ component with an `.as` one.

C++ code is linked into the executable, so a C++ project names its own CMake
target in the `.mproj`:

@code{.ini}
[build]
target = "my_game"
@endcode

The editor generates a `CMakeLists.txt` (using `mercury_add_game`) the first
time the project opens if it does not have one. Play/Build configure and build
the project's own tree in `.mercury/build/debug`; the first native build asks
for workspace trust, and an untrusted project is never configured. Passing
`--greeting` and other custom options to a game works only when an
`AppHook` is registered (see @ref cpp_api_systems).

The **New Script** dialog has four kinds:

| Kind | Files | Base |
|---|---|---|
| AngelScript Behaviour | `scripts/<snake>.as` | `[script] class ... : Behaviour` |
| C++ Behaviour | `src/<snake>.h`, `.cc` | @ref Mercury::Behaviour + `MERCURY_SCRIPT` |
| C++ System | `src/<snake>.h`, `.cc` | @ref Mercury::System + `MERCURY_SYSTEM` |
| C++ Editor System | `system/<snake>.h`, `.cc` | @ref Mercury::EditorUI::EditorExtension + `MERCURY_EDITOR_EXTENSION` |

The dialog previews the exact paths, refuses to overwrite, and **Open after
creating** opens the `.cc`/`.as` (the choice and last kind are remembered).
C++ Systems are scene-wide subsystems with `kPreTick`/`kTick`/`kPostTick`/
`kPostPhysics` phases; contact sheets, app hooks, the EventBus, render hooks
and the editor-extension lifecycle are documented in @ref cpp_api_systems and
@ref cpp_api_editor_systems.

See @ref getting_started_cpp and `examples/cpp_hello`, whose `src/` files add
a System, an AppHook reading `--greeting`, and a render hook that toggles
Filament bloom with **F2**.

@section scripting_examples Learn from the examples

The `examples/` folder is the best reference. In particular:

- `examples/pong/scripts/ball.as` shows a fixed-step behaviour with a
  `[callable]` method and randomisation.
- `examples/roll_a_ball/scripts/player_controller.as` shows input actions,
  buffering a press between `onFrame` and `onTick`, and physics forces.
- `examples/monkey_ball/` shows a 3D game with a HUD and a pause menu.
- `examples/breakout/` and `examples/space_invaders/` show 2D collisions and
  spawning.
- `examples/cpp_hello/` is the same spinning-cube idea as this page, written
  as a C++ `MERCURY_SCRIPT` component.

*/
